<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agathocles of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">361–289 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agathocles of Syracuse seized power as tyrant of Syracuse in 317 BCE and later took the title of king. Fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, he boldly invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in 310 BCE. He extended his power over much of Sicily, raided southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and held Corcyra. His Campanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the Mamertines, seized Messana after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse.jpg" alt="Agathocles of Syracuse" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stella · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moneta_Apollo_di_Agatocle_IV-III_sec._a.C.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
